## Every New Session

1. Invoke `manage-workspace-memory` implicitly for workspace startup and read this entry plus the long-term routing entry completely.
2. Resolve the active project or deliverable, then read its current status.
3. Follow only task-relevant design, asset, dependency, topic, and primary-evidence links.
4. Verify consequential state against physical artifacts before mutation.

Do not require the user to name a Skill when the task clearly matches one. During bootstrap, replace the table below with routes derived from the capabilities actually installed and relevant to this project's task types. Omit unavailable or irrelevant Skills; do not copy a suite-wide catalog mechanically.

| Project task pattern | Installed capability route | Composition or exclusion boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `<task pattern inferred from deliverables>` | `<available-skill-name>` | `<when to compose or not invoke>` |

Build each row from the installed Skill's current name and trigger description, then add project-specific entry points such as the owning status, style guide, data contract, or delivery rules. Compose matching Skills when their responsibilities genuinely cross boundaries.

Use a lifecycle orchestrator only when an upstream authority change invalidates several downstream artifacts, exact candidate/submitted/revision/release state must be governed, or multiple linked deliverables need one traceable transition. Two adjacent specialist tasks may compose directly without an orchestrator.

## Event-Driven Maintenance

During an active session, `manage-workspace-memory` records only durable changes to policy, scope, approved design, authority, dependency, blocker, current state, frozen identity, or next action. Transient commands and unchanged inspections do not trigger memory writes.

Before closing a materially changed session:

1. verify the claimed artifacts;
2. update only affected status, design, index, DAG, topic, or history authorities;
3. record a concrete first safe next action and blocker clearance conditions;
4. run the three-hop recovery check;
5. bind handoff records to the final post-metadata-edit artifacts.

Implicit invocation is not permission to move, delete, overwrite, publish, push, alter frozen material, write outside scope, or install watchers/hooks/scheduled tasks. Obtain explicit authority for those actions. “Automatic maintenance” here means that an active, matching agent session follows this contract after a durable event; it does not mean continuous background monitoring.

